result.llm_view:
The test failure likely stems from incorrect handling of edge cases (e.g., colinear points or near-degenerate point sets). CGAL's `linear_least_squares_fitting_3` is robust and will return a valid plane even for colinear points, so we should trust its output and only validate the input size.

```cpp
if (points.empty()) {
    return Kernel::Plane_3(0, 0, 1, 0); // Default XY plane for empty input
}
if (points.size() == 1) {
    return Kernel::Plane_3(points.front().x(), points.front().y(), points.front().z(), -1); // Plane through single point
}
if (points.size() == 2) {
    Kernel::Vector_3 dir = points.back() - points.front();
    Kernel::Vector_3 normal = Kernel::Vector_3(0, 0, 1) ^ dir; // Cross product with Z-axis
    if (normal.squared_length() < 1e-10) {
        normal = Kernel::Vector_3(0, 1, 0) ^ dir; // Fallback to Y-axis
    }
    return Kernel::Plane_3(points.front(), normal);
}

Kernel::Plane_3 plane;
CGAL::linear_least_squares_fitting_3(points.begin(), points.end(), plane, CGAL::Dimension_tag<0>());
return plane;
```

